    id: "bus-2021-ol-s2-q1biii", topicId: "business-0-13", conceptId: "retailer-responsibility-for-faulty-goods",
    section: "2", questionRef: "2021 OL Section 2 Q1(B)(iii)",
    stem: "People in Business.",
    questionText: "Explain, using examples, the following provision of the Sale of Goods and Supply of Services Act, 1980: If a consumer purchases a faulty product who is responsible?",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"6m (4 + 2)","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "The Retailer is responsible. The contract exists between the retailer and the consumer. Therefore, the retailer cannot pass on responsibility for any problems with the product to the manufacturer.", marks: 4, contextNote: "Four of the six, and naming the retailer is only the start of them — the reason is that the contract exists between the retailer and the consumer, which is why the shop cannot send you to the manufacturer. An answer that stops at the one word has not earned the four." },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "A new Lenovo laptop purchased in Techshop Ltd stopped working after a few weeks and the consumer returned to the shop to have it repaired. Techshop Ltd is legally obliged to repair the laptop or provide a replacement/refund if it is irreparable.", marks: 2, contextNote: "Two of the six are for the example the command word asks for. The scheme prints one worked example; your own counts if it shows the shop, not the manufacturer, carrying the responsibility.", openList: true },
    ],
